      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "Let the integer be \\( x \\). Given that \\( x \\equiv 7 \\pmod{15} \\), we can write \\( x = 15k + 7 \\) for some integer \\( k \\).\n\nWe need to find the remainders when \\( x \\) is divided by 3 and by 5. To do this, we can substitute \\( x = 15k + 7 \\) into these divisions.\n\n1. **Modulus by 3**:\n   \\[\n   x \\equiv 7 \\pmod{3}\n   \\]\n   Simplify 7 modulo 3:\n   \\[\n   7 \\divideby 3 = 2 \\quad \\text{remainder} \\quad 1\n   \\]\n   So,\n   \\[\n   7 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{3}\n   \\]\n   Therefore,\n   \\[\n   x \\equiv 1 \\pmod{3}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Modulus by 5**:\n   \\[\n   x \\equiv 7 \\pmod{5}\n   \\]\n   Simplify 7 modulo 5:\n   \\[\n   7 = 5 \\times 1 + 2\n   \\]\n   So,\n   \\[\n   7 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5}\n   \\]\n   Therefore,\n   \\[\n   x \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5}\n   \\]\n\nNow, sum the remainders:\n\\[\n1 + 2 = 3\n\\]\n\nHence, the sum of the remainders when the same integer is divided by 3 and by 5 is \\(\\boxed{3}\\).",
