      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "Given the integer \\( x \\) such that when \\( x \\) is divided by 15, the remainder is 7, we can express this relationship mathematically as:\n\n\\[ x \\equiv 7 \\pmod{15} \\]\n\nThis implies:\n\n\\[ x = 15k + 7 \\]\nfor some integer \\( k \\).\n\nNext, we need to find the remainders when \\( x \\) is divided by 3 and by 5.\n\nFirst, consider the remainder when \\( x \\) is divided by 3:\n\\[ x = 15k + 7 \\]\n\nSince 15 is a multiple of 3, we have:\n\n\\[ 15k \\equiv 0 \\pmod{3} \\]\n\nSo, the remainder when \\( x \\) is divided by 3 is the same as the remainder when 7 is divided by 3:\n\n\\[ x \\equiv 7 \\pmod{3} \\]\n\\[ 7 \\div 3 = 2 \\quad \\text{remainder} \\quad 1 \\]\n\nThus,\n\n\\[ 7 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{3} \\]\n\nTherefore, the remainder when \\( x \\) is divided by 3 is 1.\n\nNext, we consider the remainder when \\( x \\) is divided by 5:\n\\[ x = 15k + 7 \\]\n\nSince 15 is a multiple of 5, we have:\n\n\\[ 15k \\equiv 0 \\pmod{5} \\]\n\nSo, the remainder when \\( x \\) is divided by 5 is the same as the remainder when 7 is divided by 5:\n\n\\[ x \\equiv 7 \\pmod{5} \\]\n\\[ 7 \\div 5 = 1 \\quad \\text{remainder} \\quad 2 \\]\n\nThus,\n\n\\[ 7 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5} \\]\n\nTherefore, the remainder when \\( x \\) is divided by 5 is 2.\n\nFinally, we sum the remainders when \\( x \\) is divided by 3 and by 5:\n\n\\[ 1 + 2 = 3 \\]\n\nHence, the sum of the remainders is:\n\n\\[\\boxed{3}\\]",
